rows=[]
def q(*a,**k):rows.append(question(*a,**k))

q('ALT is 600 U/L (ULN 40) and alkaline phosphatase is 300 U/L (ULN 120). What is the R value and biochemical pattern?', 'R = 6; hepatocellular pattern',[
 ('R = 2; cholestatic pattern','Dividing raw values ignores their different reference limits. R uses multiples of each enzyme’s ULN.'),('R = 2.5; mixed pattern','2.5 is the ALP multiple of normal, not the ratio of the two normalized values.'),('R = 15; hepatocellular pattern','15 is the ALT multiple of normal; the ALP multiple must still be included.')],
 'Normalize each enzyme before comparing the injury pattern', '**R = (ALT/ALT ULN) ÷ (ALP/ALP ULN) = 15 ÷ 2.5 = 6.**', ['An R value of at least 5 indicates a hepatocellular pattern.','A value at or below 2 is cholestatic; between 2 and 5 is mixed. The exact boundary convention should be stated when relevant.'], '**A pattern narrows the differential but does not establish the cause or severity.**', ['Viral, ischemic, autoimmune and drug-related injury can share this pattern.','Bilirubin, INR, symptoms and time course determine urgency; an isolated ratio does not.'], [['R ≥5','Hepatocellular'],['R ≤2','Cholestatic'],['2<R<5','Mixed']], [ACG,AAS])
q('Which pair most directly reflects hepatocellular injury rather than hepatic synthetic capacity?', 'ALT and AST',[
 ('Albumin and INR','These are commonly used to assess aspects of hepatic synthesis, subject to important confounders.'),('INR and factor V activity','Both can reflect synthesis and are influenced by clinical context.'),('Albumin and fibrinogen','These are synthesized proteins rather than leakage enzymes of hepatocellular injury.')],
 'The conventional label “liver function tests” hides different biological domains', '**Aminotransferases indicate cell injury; they do not directly quantify remaining functional liver mass.**', ['Marked enzyme elevation can occur with recoverable injury.','Advanced cirrhosis can exist with only modest enzyme abnormalities.'], '**Assess injury and function together.**', ['INR can be confounded by anticoagulants or vitamin K deficiency.','Albumin is affected by inflammation, distribution, renal or gut losses and its relatively long half-life.'], [['ALT/AST','Cell injury'],['Albumin/INR','Synthetic-function clues with confounders'],['Bilirubin','Production, handling and excretion']], [ACG,AAS])
q('An asymptomatic patient has ALP 420 U/L but normal bilirubin and aminotransferases. Which additional laboratory test helps establish whether the ALP is hepatobiliary in origin?', 'GGT or ALP isoenzyme fractionation',[
 ('Serum creatine kinase as the primary ALP-source test','CK helps assess muscle injury in an aminotransferase problem, not the usual ALP-source question.'),('Serum haptoglobin','This is used in a hemolysis assessment rather than ALP-source confirmation.'),('Serum amylase','This does not reliably distinguish bone from hepatobiliary ALP.')],
 'Confirm the tissue source before designing a cholestasis work-up', '**ALP can arise from liver, bone, placenta and other tissues.**', ['An accompanying GGT elevation supports a hepatobiliary source.','GGT itself is nonspecific and should not be used indiscriminately to screen for liver disease.'], '**A confirmed hepatic ALP elevation warrants etiological assessment.**', ['Ultrasound can look for duct dilation and structural disease.','A nonhepatic source redirects evaluation toward the relevant tissue rather than automatic biliary imaging.'], [['ALP plus GGT','Supports hepatobiliary source'],['ALP with normal GGT','Consider nonhepatic sources'],['Isoenzymes','Alternative source clarification']], [ACG], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A patient has pruritus, conjugated hyperbilirubinemia and a disproportionately elevated hepatic ALP. What is the usual first imaging test?', 'Right upper quadrant ultrasonography',[
 ('Diagnostic ERCP in every stable patient before noninvasive imaging','ERCP carries procedural risk and is usually reserved for an actionable therapeutic or selected diagnostic indication.'),('Liver biopsy before assessing duct caliber','A potentially extrahepatic obstruction should generally be assessed noninvasively first.'),('A plain abdominal radiograph as sufficient exclusion of obstruction','It does not adequately assess the biliary tree.')],
 'First separate an obstructed large-duct pathway from intrahepatic cholestasis', '**Ultrasound can identify duct dilation, gallstones and some structural lesions.**', ['A nondilated tree does not exclude every early or partial obstruction.','The quality of visualization and the clinical probability determine whether MRCP or EUS is needed.'], '**Urgency depends on the clinical syndrome.**', ['Cholangitis with sepsis calls for prompt drainage planning rather than a leisurely sequence.','Stable persistent cholestasis without dilation may require autoimmune, drug and infiltrative evaluation.'], [['Initial stable assessment','Ultrasound'],['Persistent duct concern','MRCP/EUS as appropriate'],['Cholangitis','Urgent therapeutic drainage pathway']], [ACG,AAS], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A young adult has bilirubin 2.8 mg/dL, predominantly unconjugated, normal ALT/ALP and no anemia or reticulocytosis. Episodes occur with fasting and intercurrent illness. What is the most likely diagnosis?', 'Gilbert syndrome',[
 ('Dubin–Johnson syndrome','This causes predominantly conjugated rather than unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia.'),('Warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia','Hemolysis would usually have supporting hematological findings such as reticulocytosis, low haptoglobin or anemia.'),('Extrahepatic biliary obstruction','This usually produces a conjugated/cholestatic pattern rather than this isolated benign profile.')],
 'An isolated indirect fraction suggests production or conjugation mechanisms', '**Reduced UGT1A1-mediated conjugation produces mild fluctuating unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia in Gilbert syndrome.**', ['Fasting, illness, dehydration and exertion can accentuate the bilirubin.','The remainder of the liver profile and hemolysis assessment should fit the diagnosis.'], '**Avoid unnecessary invasive testing in a characteristic uncomplicated presentation.**', ['Explain the benign course and relevant drug-metabolism implications.','Atypical levels, mixed abnormalities or a changed clinical course require reassessment.'], [['Gilbert','Mild indirect, no hemolysis'],['Hemolysis','Indirect plus hematological evidence'],['Dubin–Johnson/Rotor','Predominantly direct']], [ACG,GIL], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A patient has predominantly unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia, anemia, reticulocytosis, high LDH and low haptoglobin. Which mechanism best explains the jaundice?', 'Increased bilirubin production from hemolysis',[
 ('Isolated reduced conjugation from Gilbert syndrome as the sole explanation','Gilbert syndrome does not itself cause anemia with this hemolytic marker pattern.'),('Canalicular conjugated-bilirubin export failure','This would favor direct hyperbilirubinemia and does not explain the hemolytic markers.'),('Large-duct biliary obstruction','That does not fit the predominantly indirect fraction and erythrocyte-destruction findings.')],
 'Use the blood count and turnover markers to identify the source', '**Heme breakdown increases the bilirubin load presented to the liver.**', ['Reticulocytosis reflects marrow response when adequate.','Haptoglobin can be affected by synthesis and inflammation, so the full constellation matters.'], '**Investigate the cause of hemolysis separately.**', ['The direct antiglobulin test, blood film and exposure history can refine the differential.','A person can also have Gilbert syndrome, which may amplify the bilirubin during hemolysis.'], [['Hemolysis','Production increased'],['Gilbert','Conjugation capacity reduced'],['Cholestasis','Excretion impaired']], [ACG], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Why is bilirubin usually absent from urine in isolated unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia?', 'Unconjugated bilirubin is albumin-bound and not freely filtered into urine',[
 ('It has already been fully converted to water-soluble glucuronides','That describes conjugated bilirubin rather than the isolated unconjugated state.'),('The kidney conjugates all bilirubin before filtering it','Hepatic conjugation is the central physiological pathway.'),('Any jaundice necessarily produces a positive urine bilirubin test','Urinary bilirubin depends on the fraction and its filterability.')],
 'Water solubility and protein binding determine urinary handling', '**Conjugation makes bilirubin water-soluble; unconjugated bilirubin circulates tightly bound to albumin.**', ['A urine bilirubin result can therefore help interpret a jaundice pattern.','A dark urine color has other causes, including hemoglobin and myoglobin.'], '**Do not infer the pigment from color alone.**', ['Use fractionated serum bilirubin and the urinalysis.','Myoglobinuria after muscle injury is a different diagnostic problem.'], [['Unconjugated bilirubin','Usually no bilirubinuria'],['Conjugated bilirubin','Can produce bilirubinuria'],['Dark urine','Requires pigment-specific interpretation']], [ACG])
q('A patient has lifelong mild conjugated hyperbilirubinemia, normal liver enzymes and a darkly pigmented liver. Which defect is characteristic?', 'ABCC2/MRP2-mediated canalicular export impairment in Dubin–Johnson syndrome',[
 ('UGT1A1 deficiency','This causes impaired conjugation and an unconjugated pattern.'),('Combined OATP1B1 and OATP1B3 deficiency','This causes Rotor syndrome, typically without the dark liver pigment.'),('ATP7B dysfunction','This causes Wilson disease rather than the stated benign conjugated-hyperbilirubinemia syndrome.')],
 'Distinguish benign transport disorders by the step affected', '**Dubin–Johnson syndrome impairs canalicular excretion of conjugated organic anions.**', ['Conjugation can be intact despite a predominantly direct bilirubin elevation.','The classic dark hepatic pigment distinguishes it from Rotor syndrome.'], '**Confirm a characteristic benign pattern without unnecessary invasive testing.**', ['Imaging and further evaluation depend on whether other abnormalities suggest acquired disease.','The syndrome does not explain every later cholestatic enzyme rise or new symptom.'], [['Dubin–Johnson','ABCC2/MRP2, dark pigment'],['Rotor','OATP1B1/OATP1B3, no dark pigment'],['Gilbert','UGT1A1, indirect bilirubin']], [ROT], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Which urinary coproporphyrin pattern most favors Rotor syndrome over Dubin–Johnson syndrome?', 'Increased total urinary coproporphyrins with an isomer I fraction around 65%',[
 ('Normal total coproporphyrins with an isomer I fraction above 80%','This is the more typical Dubin–Johnson pattern.'),('Absent urinary coproporphyrins in both syndromes','Neither syndrome is defined by complete absence.'),('A normal isomer distribution with isolated unconjugated bilirubin','This is not the characteristic Rotor transport phenotype.')],
 'Total excretion and isomer distribution answer different questions', '**Rotor syndrome reflects combined hepatic uptake/reuptake transporter dysfunction.**', ['Total urinary coproporphyrin excretion increases, often several-fold.','Dubin–Johnson usually has a relatively normal total with a higher proportion of isomer I.'], '**Use the biochemical distinction in a compatible clinical setting.**', ['Both can produce benign predominantly conjugated hyperbilirubinemia.','An acquired cholestatic syndrome should not be dismissed solely because a transport disorder is suspected.'], [['Rotor','Total increased; isomer I around 65%'],['Dubin–Johnson','Total usually normal; isomer I >80%'],['Clinical pattern','Usually otherwise preserved liver profile']], [ROT])
q('Which finding most strongly favors Crigler–Najjar type II over type I?', 'A substantial bilirubin response to phenobarbital due to residual UGT1A1 activity',[
 ('Complete absence of conjugating enzyme activity','That is characteristic of the more severe type I end of the spectrum.'),('Predominantly conjugated hyperbilirubinemia','Both Crigler–Najjar forms primarily affect unconjugated bilirubin.'),('Canalicular MRP2 dysfunction with a dark liver','That describes Dubin–Johnson syndrome.')],
 'Residual enzyme activity changes severity and inducibility', '**Type II retains some bilirubin-conjugating capacity, which can be induced pharmacologically.**', ['Type I usually presents with severe neonatal unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia and high neurotoxicity risk.','Phenotypic severity and molecular findings guide specialist management.'], '**Do not extrapolate benign Gilbert management to severe inherited unconjugated disease.**', ['Phototherapy and definitive therapies may be required in severe disease.','Neurological risk depends on bilirubin exposure and clinical context.'], [['Type I','Absent/near-absent activity, severe disease'],['Type II','Residual activity, phenobarbital response'],['Gilbert','Much milder common phenotype']], [ref('NHGRI Clinical Genomic Database: UGT1A1','https://research.nhgri.nih.gov/CGD/view/?g=UGT1A1')])
q('After prolonged obstructive jaundice resolves, direct bilirubin remains elevated while symptoms, ALP and free conjugated bilirubin improve. Which phenomenon can contribute?', 'Delta bilirubin, consisting of conjugated bilirubin covalently bound to albumin',[
 ('A new increase in unconjugated bilirubin caused only by hemolysis','That would not explain a persistently direct-reacting albumin-bound fraction.'),('A mandatory persistent mechanical obstruction in every such case','Persistent direct bilirubin needs context; delta bilirubin can lag after resolution.'),('Rapid renal filtration of all albumin-bound bilirubin','Albumin binding prevents such rapid clearance.')],
 'A measured direct fraction can outlast the original obstruction', '**Delta bilirubin clears with albumin turnover rather than with the short kinetics of free conjugated bilirubin.**', ['It can account for a slower decline after substantial conjugated hyperbilirubinemia.','This explanation is appropriate only when the rest of the clinical course supports recovery.'], '**Do not use the concept to ignore worsening cholestasis.**', ['Recurrent pain, fever, rising ALP or imaging concern can still require renewed evaluation.','The trend and other findings matter more than one persistent value.'], [['Free conjugated bilirubin','Relatively prompt clearance when excretion recovers'],['Delta bilirubin','Albumin-associated persistence'],['Worsening clinical course','Investigate ongoing disease']], [ref('Prolonged Direct Hyperbilirubinemia Following Acute Hepatitis','https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11622201/')], 'clinical_scenario')
q('After unaccustomed intense exercise, AST is 680 U/L, ALT 210 U/L, CK 25,000 U/L and bilirubin/ALP are normal. What is the best initial interpretation?', 'Skeletal muscle injury is a major plausible source of the aminotransferase elevations',[
 ('The AST/ALT ratio alone proves alcohol-associated hepatitis','A ratio is not specific and the CK/exposure pattern points to muscle injury.'),('Any ALT rise excludes a muscle source','ALT can also increase with substantial skeletal muscle damage.'),('Normal bilirubin excludes rhabdomyolysis','Bilirubin is not the diagnostic marker for muscle breakdown.')],
 'Aminotransferases are not perfectly liver-specific', '**Marked CK elevation with muscle exposure and compatible symptoms supports a muscle contribution.**', ['Assess renal function, potassium and the severity of rhabdomyolysis.','A coexisting hepatic injury remains possible if the trajectory or other findings do not fit.'], '**Follow the clinical and biochemical evolution.**', ['Concordant CK and aminotransferase improvement supports the interpretation.','Do not delay urgent muscle-injury treatment while pursuing an assumed liver-only diagnosis.'], [['CK','Muscle-injury marker'],['AST/ALT','Can rise from muscle'],['Bilirubin/ALP','Help assess a concurrent hepatobiliary pattern']], [ref('AAST Critical Care Committee Consensus: Rhabdomyolysis','https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8804685/')], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A patient has profound hypotension followed by AST and ALT above 3,000 U/L, a marked LDH rise and then a rapid enzyme decline after perfusion improves. Which diagnosis is most likely?', 'Hypoxic/ischemic hepatitis',[
 ('Uncomplicated Gilbert syndrome','This does not produce massive aminotransferase elevation.'),('Primary biliary cholangitis as the most typical pattern','PBC is usually a chronic cholestatic rather than abrupt shock-related massive hepatocellular injury.'),('A stable benign conjugated-bilirubin transport defect','That does not explain the acute enzyme and hemodynamic pattern.')],
 'The perfusion event and trajectory are central clues', '**Hypoxic injury can cause dramatic aminotransferase and LDH elevations.**', ['Cardiac failure, respiratory failure or sepsis may contribute even without a clearly recorded prolonged hypotensive episode.','Acetaminophen toxicity and acute viral or autoimmune causes remain important alternatives in massive injury.'], '**Treat the underlying circulatory or oxygen-delivery problem.**', ['Monitor INR, glucose, lactate and renal function.','An enzyme fall must be interpreted with function and clinical recovery, not celebrated in isolation.'], [['Shock/hypoxia plus marked LDH','Supports hypoxic injury'],['Rapid fall after stabilization','Compatible trajectory'],['INR/glucose/mental state','Assess functional consequence']], [ACG,AAS], 'clinical_scenario')
q('In acute severe hepatocellular injury, aminotransferases fall rapidly but INR rises and encephalopathy develops. What is the correct interpretation?', 'Worsening liver failure remains possible despite lower enzymes and requires urgent specialist/transplant-center assessment',[
 ('The enzyme fall guarantees recovery regardless of INR','Leakage enzymes can fall when viable hepatocyte mass is lost.'),('Encephalopathy is excluded if bilirubin is not very high','Bilirubin magnitude is not a requirement for encephalopathy.'),('Wait for albumin to fall before assessing severity','Albumin is too slow and confounded to be the decisive acute safety marker.')],
 'A falling injury marker and worsening organ function can coexist', '**Synthetic failure and mental status can be more urgent than the direction of ALT.**', ['Check glucose, coagulation, acid-base status and other organ dysfunction.','Exclude sedatives, hypoglycemia and other contributors to altered consciousness while treating the liver emergency.'], '**Escalate before advanced encephalopathy removes the transfer window.**', ['Acute liver failure requires early expert care.','Cause-specific treatment should proceed alongside assessment for transplant suitability.'], [['ALT/AST fall','May mean recovery or loss of viable cells'],['INR rise plus encephalopathy','Urgent failure signal'],['Action','Early expert/transplant-center discussion']], [ref('AASLD Acute Liver Failure Position Paper','https://www.aasld.org/sites/default/files/2022-07/AcuteLiverFailureUpdate201journalformat1.pdf')], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A patient with prolonged obstructive jaundice has an elevated INR that substantially corrects after parenteral vitamin K. What does this support?', 'Vitamin K deficiency from impaired fat-soluble vitamin absorption contributed to the coagulopathy',[
 ('Definitive proof that all hepatocyte synthetic capacity is normal','Correction supports a deficiency contribution but does not exclude coexisting liver disease.'),('Proof of a congenital factor V deficiency','Factor V is not vitamin K-dependent and would not explain this response.'),('A mandatory need for fresh frozen plasma in every asymptomatic case','Plasma decisions depend on bleeding/procedure context and should not follow an isolated number automatically.')],
 'Cholestasis can impair vitamin delivery as well as bilirubin excretion', '**Reduced intestinal bile delivery can impair fat and fat-soluble vitamin absorption.**', ['Vitamin K-dependent coagulation factors may fall.','Parenteral replacement bypasses the impaired intestinal absorption route.'], '**Interpret a response as one piece of the functional assessment.**', ['Persistent INR elevation after appropriate replacement needs further evaluation.','Medication-related anticoagulation and other causes must be considered.'], [['Vitamin K response','Supports deficiency contribution'],['No meaningful correction','Consider synthetic failure/other causes'],['Factor V','Not vitamin K-dependent']], [ACG,AAS], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A patient has mildly elevated ALT for a year, metabolic risk factors and normal bilirubin. Which statement is most accurate?', 'Mild enzyme elevation does not exclude clinically important fibrosis; etiological and fibrosis-risk assessment are still appropriate',[
 ('ALT below twice normal excludes advanced fibrosis','Enzyme height is not a reliable fibrosis stage.'),('Normal bilirubin proves the liver is histologically normal','Bilirubin can remain normal in compensated advanced disease.'),('Repeated observation alone is always sufficient regardless of risk factors','A persistent abnormality should be evaluated in context rather than repeatedly deferred.')],
 'Magnitude of biochemical injury is not a fibrosis measurement', '**Chronic liver disease can progress with modest or intermittently normal aminotransferases.**', ['Metabolic risk does not exclude viral, autoimmune, iron-related or drug causes.','Use appropriate noninvasive fibrosis assessment in the relevant disease pathway.'], '**Investigate the cause and the stage as separate questions.**', ['A diagnosis of steatosis does not establish whether advanced fibrosis is present.','Management intensity depends on both disease activity and long-term risk.'], [['ALT level','Injury clue'],['Etiological assessment','Identifies cause'],['Fibrosis assessment','Estimates stage/risk']], [BSG,ACG], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Which laboratory pattern is most consistent with cholestatic injury?', 'ALP elevation proportionally greater than ALT, after confirming a hepatobiliary ALP source',[
 ('ALT twenty times ULN with ALP 1.2 times ULN','This is predominantly hepatocellular.'),('Isolated indirect bilirubin elevation with normal enzymes','This suggests a bilirubin production/conjugation problem rather than cholestatic enzyme injury.'),('AST elevation with very high CK after exertion','A muscle source is a major alternative.')],
 'Compare relative elevation and verify tissue origin', '**Cholestasis is an impairment of bile formation or flow and can be intrahepatic or extrahepatic.**', ['Bilirubin need not be elevated early.','GGT can help confirm hepatic ALP but does not identify the cause.'], '**Use anatomy and history to refine the differential.**', ['Ultrasound is a common initial test.','Medicines, autoimmune duct disease and infiltrative processes are relevant when obstruction is not established.'], [['Disproportionate hepatic ALP','Cholestatic pattern'],['Disproportionate ALT','Hepatocellular pattern'],['Isolated indirect bilirubin','Different mechanism']], [ACG,AAS])
q('An asymptomatic adult has a newly discovered minor liver chemistry abnormality. Which is the best general approach?', 'Confirm the abnormality and undertake a proportionate history, medication review and etiological assessment rather than serially repeating tests without a plan',[
 ('Repeat indefinitely until the result becomes normal, regardless of risks','This can delay recognition of chronic disease.'),('Proceed directly to liver biopsy before any history or imaging','Biopsy is not the universal first step.'),('Ignore all prescribed and nonprescribed products if the patient drinks no alcohol','Medicines, supplements and other exposures remain relevant.')],
 'Confirmation is useful only when paired with a diagnostic strategy', '**The pattern, magnitude, duration, symptoms and risk factors guide the next steps.**', ['Review alcohol, metabolic factors, viral risks, prescriptions and supplements.','Clarifying tests can identify nonhepatic sources or a fraction-specific bilirubin problem.'], '**Escalate immediately when the clinical situation is severe.**', ['Jaundice with coagulopathy, systemic illness or encephalopathy is not a routine repeat-later scenario.','Biopsy may be useful when noninvasive evaluation remains inconclusive and the result would alter management.'], [['Minor incidental result','Confirm and evaluate proportionately'],['Persistent abnormality','Avoid endless repeat-only strategy'],['Acute dysfunction/alarm findings','Urgent assessment']], [ACG,BSG], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A septic patient develops predominantly conjugated hyperbilirubinemia with only modest aminotransferase elevations and no biliary obstruction on appropriate imaging. What mechanism is plausible?', 'Sepsis-associated intrahepatic cholestasis',[
 ('Gilbert syndrome as the direct explanation for the conjugated fraction','Gilbert produces predominantly unconjugated bilirubin.'),('Complete biliary obstruction proved by bilirubin alone despite the other findings','Bilirubin is not an anatomical obstruction test.'),('Hemolysis as the sole explanation without an indirect fraction or supporting markers','Hemolysis predominantly increases unconjugated bilirubin.')],
 'Systemic inflammation can impair bile handling', '**Sepsis can alter hepatocellular uptake and canalicular transport without a large-duct blockage.**', ['Assess for drug effects, ischemia and coexisting hepatobiliary infection.','A nondilated biliary tree must still be interpreted in relation to the clinical likelihood of obstruction.'], '**Treat the infection and organ dysfunction while monitoring the trend.**', ['Do not attribute a possible cholangitis source to generic sepsis cholestasis without assessment.','Persistent or worsening abnormalities after recovery need reconsideration.'], [['Sepsis cholestasis','Intrahepatic transport dysfunction'],['Hypoxic hepatitis','More marked hepatocellular/LDH pattern'],['Cholangitis','Biliary infection requiring source control']], [ACG,AAS], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A patient has persistently elevated isolated AST, normal ALT, normal CK and no clinical liver disease after a suitable evaluation. What laboratory explanation can be considered before invasive testing?', 'Macro-AST, an immunoglobulin-associated enzyme complex with prolonged circulation',[
 ('Acute ischemic hepatitis as established by isolated stable AST','The stable isolated pattern does not match the typical acute massive injury syndrome.'),('Biliary obstruction as proven despite normal ALP and bilirubin','The pattern does not establish a cholestatic process.'),('Gilbert syndrome as the direct cause of AST elevation','Gilbert affects bilirubin conjugation, not isolated AST circulation.')],
 'An unusual isolated laboratory abnormality may have a benign analytical/clearance explanation', '**Macroenzymes can persist because a high-molecular-weight complex clears slowly.**', ['Discuss confirmatory methods such as polyethylene glycol precipitation with the laboratory.','First assess more common hepatic, muscle and hemolysis-related explanations.'], '**Avoid a premature invasive investigation when the result and clinical picture are discordant.**', ['The finding should be confirmed rather than presumed.','New symptoms or additional abnormalities require a fresh assessment.'], [['Isolated persistent AST','Consider nonhepatic and macroenzyme causes'],['Laboratory confirmation','Specialized testing'],['Broader abnormalities','Reassess the disease differential']], [ref('Laboratory Detection of Macro-AST: Case Report and PEG Method Evaluation','https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22429521/'),ACG])

